Rotator Cuff Pathology (Tear/Tendinopathy)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lateral deltoid pain, Weakness in external rotation/abduction, Positive Empty Can test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-053
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of shoulder pain resulting in total sleep deprivation and a sudden, alarming inability to lift the arm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing severe, unrelenting nocturnal shoulder pain and an acute, distressing inability to raise their arm against gravity. Urgent assessment was strictly required to definitively differentiate an acute, massive full-thickness rotator cuff tear requiring prompt surgical repair from an acute calcific tendinitis flare or frozen shoulder, and to provide potent analgesia to restore sleep prior to daytime imaging.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shoulder has been aching for weeks, but tonight the pain is unbearable, and when I try to lift my arm to the side, it just collapses and drops."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of right shoulder pain and sudden functional weakness. Timeline: Insidious onset of anterolateral shoulder pain radiating distally into the lateral deltoid insertion over the past month. Exacerbation of deep aching pain occurred yesterday following repetitive overhead activities (painting a ceiling). Tonight, the patient experienced unrelenting nocturnal pain, explicitly provoked by rolling onto the affected shoulder, preventing any sleep. Crucially, the patient reports a sudden, alarming weakness today; they are unable to actively lift the arm out to the side (abduction). Reports feeling crepitus and catching sensations within the subacromial space during attempted arm elevation.
Functional Status: Acutely impaired right upper limb; unable to perform overhead reaching, lifting, or basic grooming tasks (e.g., brushing hair).
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation and sudden loss of functional independence in the dominant arm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly exhausted, supporting the right elbow with the contralateral hand to offload the shoulder.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right shoulder reveals no gross deformity or massive visible atrophy (suggesting an acute rather than chronic massive tear). Passive range of motion is full but painful at the extremes. Active range of motion is severely restricted: the patient cannot actively abduct the arm past 45 degrees without hiking the scapula (shrugging). Objective weakness is distinctly noted during resisted active abduction and external rotation. Positive Drop Arm test: when the arm is passively elevated to 90 degrees and released, the patient is unable to smoothly control the lowering of the arm, and it drops suddenly with pain. Positive Empty Can test: isolates and elicits severe pain and weakness within the supraspinatus muscle belly. A painful arc of active motion is noted between 60 and 120 degrees when passively assisted.
Environmental Observations: Painting supplies visible in the home, confirming the repetitive overhead mechanical overload.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg with zero relief of the severe, sharp mechanical pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Acute Full-Thickness Rotator Cuff Tear (Supraspinatus) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Frozen Shoulder (Adhesive Capsulitis) - Ruled out definitively by the preservation of a full passive range of motion (especially passive external rotation), whereas frozen shoulder has a firm, mechanical block to passive movement.

Acute Calcific Tendinitis - Possible cause of the extreme pain flare, but the distinct, objective drop-arm weakness strongly points to a structural failure (tear) rather than just severe inflammation.

Cervical Radiculopathy (C5) - Ruled out by normal biceps reflexes, intact dermatomal sensation, negative Spurling's test, and pain strictly localized to the shoulder/deltoid rather than shooting down the arm to the hand.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, sleep-depriving pain crisis and a sudden, acute loss of active shoulder abduction following overhead mechanical overload. The critical clinical imperative was to differentiate a benign tendinitis flare from a structural full-thickness tendon rupture. A rigorous physical examination, explicitly demonstrating a positive Drop Arm test and severe weakness on isolated supraspinatus testing (Empty Can) with preserved passive motion, strongly confirmed an acute structural failure (tear). The severity of the pain and sudden loss of function caused extreme distress. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided diagnostic clarity, ruled out cervical nerve compression, and allowed for the immediate application of mechanical offloading (sling) and targeted analgesia to manage the acute structural crisis prior to urgent surgical review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5-7 days. Provided a short 3-day course of Codeine 30mg PRN strictly for nocturnal use to allow sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided broad arm sling strictly for all waking hours over the next 48 hours. Consequence: The sling physically carries the weight of the arm, completely bypassing the torn supraspinatus tendon and immediately halting the sharp, tearing pain spikes (negative reinforcement).
Action: Sleep in a recliner chair or propped up on multiple pillows, placing a small folded towel under the armpit of the bad shoulder. Consequence: Prevents the shoulder from dropping backward and compressing the torn tendon, eliminating the agonizing pain that wakes you up when rolling over (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a standard poly-sling.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to arrange an urgent non-contrast shoulder MRI or diagnostic ultrasound, followed by a rapid referral to an orthopedic surgeon for consideration of acute repair.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the shoulder suddenly becomes intensely hot, red, and swollen, if a high fever develops, or if there is a sudden, total numbness or severe weakness in the entire arm or hand.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis strongly suspects a structural tendon tear based on the physical loss of strength. Urgent imaging (Ultrasound/MRI) is mandatory to confirm the size of the tear and determine if surgery is required. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will improve over 1-2 weeks with the sling and rest, but the physical inability to lift the arm sideways will not improve until the tendon is surgically reattached or extensively rehabilitated.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the entire shoulder becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you completely lose feeling in your arm or hand.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The physical testing strongly suggests that you have fully torn the main lifting tendon (supraspinatus) in your shoulder. Because the tendon is disconnected, your brain is sending the signal to lift, but the muscle has nothing to pull on, causing the arm to drop. The sling is mandatory right now to support the arm and stop the torn edges from stretching and causing severe pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to lift the arm to the side or overhead. Keep the sling on during the day. Sleep propped up tonight to protect the joint. You must see your regular doctor immediately to organize an urgent scan and a surgical referral.

